## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design branchless bit tricks for palindrome detection on 64-bit values, a common bitboard problem.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR-based bit reversal
- Branchless boolean results
- Fixed-width masking patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverse64(n) {
  n = ((n >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  n = ((n >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  n = ((n >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  n = ((n >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  n = (n >> 32n) | (n << 32n);
  return n;
}
function isPalindrome64(n) {
  return reverse64(n) === n;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests depth in bit twiddling and constant-time operations; parity computation is fundamental in checksums, error detection, cryptography, and performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts
- Parity reduction across word boundaries by folding upper and lower halves
- Fixed-time parallel reduction using shifts and XOR
- No branches; constant-time execution regardless of input

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t p = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  p ^= p >> 32;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to arbitrary bit widths or for streaming parity computation?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compare 128-bit values split across lanes and derive a scalar metric without 128-bit math.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit data split into hi/lo lanes
- XOR to highlight differences
- 64-bit popcount for bit counting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Reference approach in C-like pseudocode; interview would use C/C++
int hammingDistance128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo) {
  uint64_t dh = a_hi ^ b_hi;
  uint64_t dl = a_lo ^ b_lo;
  return __builtin_popcountll(dh) + __builtin_popcountll(dl);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to implement popcount without builtins?
- What if values are stored in different endianness?
- How would this extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design constant-time bit-manipulation without branches for 128-bit values, a common low-latency constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-reversal techniques
- 128-bit word handling
- Branchless bit tricks

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x=(x>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL | (x&0x5555555555555555ULL)<<1;
  x=(x>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL | (x&0x3333333333333333ULL)<<2;
  x=(x>>4)&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL | (x&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL)<<4;
  x=(x>>8)&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL | (x&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL)<<8;
  x=(x>>16)&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL | (x&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL)<<16;
  x=(x>>32)&0x00000000ffffffffULL | (x&0x00000000ffffffffULL)<<32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.lo); r.hi = reverse64(x.hi); uint64_t t=r.lo; r.lo=r.hi; r.hi=t; return r; }
unsigned isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x){ uint128 r = reverse128(x); return (x.hi==r.hi && x.lo==r.lo) ? 1 : 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to arbitrary width? 
- How does endianness affect input from memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a branchless bit-packing operation across 128 bits—a common low-level optimization used in compression algorithms, bitmap libraries, and SIMD data processing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit masks and shifts
- Branchless algorithms
- Parallel bit extraction and compression

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 compress128(uint128 x, uint128 mask) {
    // Stage 1: compact adjacent bits (shift 1)
    uint128 m = mask & ~(mask << 1);
    x = (x & m) | ((x >> 1) & ~m);
    mask &= ~(mask << 1);

    // Subsequent stages (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) follow the same pattern
    // with appropriate shift amounts...

    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests facility with non-trivial bit tricks on 128-bit values, without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Per-byte masking and bit propagation
- Operating on 128-bit lanes with fixed-width shifts
